-- title:
--   Lemma 1 — the posterior means of $\varepsilon_a$, $\varepsilon_s$ and $f_j$ given $f_i$
-- statement:
--   Let firm $i$ have rival $j$, let $\theta>0$, and let $f_i=\varepsilon_a+\varepsilon_i$ be firm $i$'s demand signal. Then, almost surely,
--
--   $$
--   E[\varepsilon_a\mid f_i]=\lambda_if_i,\qquad E[\varepsilon_s\mid f_i]=\rho\theta\lambda_if_i,\qquad E[f_j\mid f_i]=(1+\beta)\lambda_if_i.
--   $$
--
--   These are the demand-side updating, the cost-side updating and the inference about the rival's signal. Together they reduce a firm's best response to a linear equation in its signal. The second identity is the source of the price-learning effect: it is the only channel through which the price volatility $\theta$ enters the equilibrium.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: jointly distributed normal noises on an unnamed probability space → the product of four standard normals on ℝ⁴, with the noises as explicit linear forms in the coordinates; reason: this realizes exactly the stated joint law, and every quantity in the statement depends only on that law
--   - reading: ε_A and ε_B are independent of ε_a and ε_s and correlated with coefficient η (p. 9) → the pair (ε_A, ε_B) is bivariate normal and independent of the pair (ε_a, ε_s), all four jointly Gaussian; reason: the sentence leaves the joint law open, and E[f_j | f_i] in Lemma 1 requires joint normality; this is the reading under which the paper's formulas hold
--   - encoding: E[·|f_i] as a function of the signal → conditional expectation given the σ-algebra generated by f_i, with equalities P-almost surely; reason: the conditional expectation is defined only up to null sets
--   - encoding: θ = σ_s/σ_a, the ratio of two standard deviations, with σ_s the standard deviation of a nondegenerate normal correlated ρ with ε_a (p. 9, p. 11) → θ is a real argument with hypothesis 0 < θ, and σ_s := θσ_a; reason: the paper's θ is positive (σ_s > 0 is implied by the correlation coefficient ρ of (ε_a, ε_s)); the hypothesis excludes only parameters the paper does not have
-- source:
--   Xing–Liu–Zhang–Zhao, SSRN 4717149 (2023-12-27), Lemma 1, p. 11; version of record: Management Science (2024), doi:10.1287/mnsc.2023.00304

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CommodityCPC_Model

namespace CommodityCPC

open MeasureTheory

/-- **Lemma 1: the posterior means**, Xing–Liu–Zhang–Zhao, *The Bright Side of Price Volatility in
Global Commodity Procurement*, SSRN 4717149 (2023-12-27), Management Science (2024),
doi:10.1287/mnsc.2023.00304, Lemma 1, p. 11. For a firm `i` with rival `j`:
`E[ε_a | f_i] = λ_i f_i`, `E[ε_s | f_i] = ρθλ_i f_i` and `E[f_j | f_i] = (1 + β)λ_i f_i`.

**Formalization Note.** Conditioning is on `σ(f_i)`, and the equalities hold `P`-almost surely. -/
theorem lemma_1 (p : Params) {θ : ℝ} (hθ : 0 < θ) (i : Firm) :
    P[p.εa | p.signalAlgebra i] =ᵐ[P] (fun ω => p.lam i * p.signal i ω) ∧
    P[p.εs θ | p.signalAlgebra i] =ᵐ[P] (fun ω => p.ρ * θ * p.lam i * p.signal i ω) ∧
    P[p.signal i.other | p.signalAlgebra i] =ᵐ[P]
      (fun ω => (1 + p.β) * p.lam i * p.signal i ω) := by sorry

end CommodityCPC
